Q: External library stops working when the production angular app is built A: <p>Very late to the party on this one but ran into it after an upgrade to Angular 20.</p>
<p>The solution for me was to move the import BEFORE the leaflet imports.</p>
<pre><code>// before
import {
    ... // blah blah blah
} from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet.markercluster';
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// after
import 'leaflet.markercluster';
import {
    ... // blah blah blah
} from 'leaflet';
</code></pre>

Q: Python typing: overload vs TypeVar A: <p>The real powerful use of <code>overload</code> allows mypy the same type checking, without needing runtime code in your function that checks <code>isinstance</code>.  This is especially useful in cases where the result handling occurs upstream of your code (external package calls, web requests, etc).</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>@overload
def return_same_type_overload(x: str) -&gt; str:
    ...

@overload
def return_same_type_overload(x: int) -&gt; int:
    ...

def return_same_type_overload(x: str | int) -&gt; str | int:
    return api_request(x) # this call decides what to return
</code></pre>
<p>Unfortunately it's impossible to illustrate this with OP's example since the parameter and return types match.  In that specific case <strong>there is no notable difference</strong> between using a <code>TypeVar</code> and <code>@overload</code>.</p>
<p>However, I think it's worth pointing out that <code>overload</code> becomes useful in cases where the input and the output are not directly linked. That's outside the scope of the question/answer but anticipates the follow-up question &quot;so when is <code>overload</code> useful?&quot;</p>
<p>A contrived case where <code>TypeVar</code> does not suffice but <code>overload</code> does might look like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>@overload
def return_variable_overload(x: int) -&gt; str:
    ...
@overload
def return_variable_overload(x: str) -&gt; str:
    ...
@overload
def return_variable_overload(x: None) -&gt; int:
    ...
@overload
def return_variable_overload(x: list[str]) -&gt; dict[str, str]:
    ...
def return_variable_overload(x: int | str | None | list[str]) -&gt; str | int | dict[str, str]:
    ...
</code></pre>

Q: Read JSON in UpperCamelCase (Pascal case) using Jsonx.formatCaseClass from play-json-extensions A: <p>Not sure if this is the best approach, but couldn't find another way.</p>
<p>When you import <code>import ai.x.play.json.Encoders.encoder</code>, it brings some implicits to do the serialization/deserialization. I removed it, and create it a custom one.</p>
<pre class="lang-scala prettyprint-override"><code>import ai.x.play.json.Jsonx
import play.api.libs.json.OFormat

case class MyType(firstKey: String, secondKey: Int)

// new NameEncoder
class CamelCaseEncoder extends BaseNameEncoder {
  override def encode(str: String): String = str.capitalize
}

implicit val encoder: NameEncoder = new CamelCaseEncoder
implicit lazy val jsonFormat: OFormat[MyType] = Jsonx.formatCaseClass[MyType]

// your test
import play.api.libs.json.Json

val json = &quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;FirstKey&quot;: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;SecondKey&quot;: 1}&quot;&quot;&quot;
val scalaObject = Json.parse(json).validate[MyType].get // MyType(&quot;a&quot;, 1)
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>For some reason, <a href="https://github.com/bizzabo/play-json-extensions/blob/ec3a07e5a0953ead7bb70f604bd626bb2bda2f69/src/main/scala/play-json.scala#L493-L495" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NameEncoder</a> is defined as a <code>sealed trait</code></p>
<pre class="lang-scala prettyprint-override"><code>sealed trait NameEncoder {
  def encode( str: String ): String
}
</code></pre>
<p>That means the trait can't be extended outside that file, meaning that can't be extended outside the library. That's why I created new class extending an existing class. Another option could be like this</p>
<pre class="lang-scala prettyprint-override"><code>trait CamelCaseEncoder { nameEncoder: NameEncoder =&gt;
  override def encode(str: String): String = str.capitalize
}
</code></pre>
<p>I copy pasted the encoding logic from <a href="https://github.com/playframework/play-json/blob/15bdcf1e8e525e44e270e23164584b0d8109be86/play-json/shared/src/main/scala/play/api/libs/json/JsonConfiguration.scala#L131-L139" rel="nofollow noreferrer">play json - JsonNaming -&gt; PascalCase</a></p>
<pre class="lang-scala prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * For each class property, use the pascal case equivalent
 * to name its column (e.g. fooBar -&gt; FooBar).
 */
object PascalCase extends JsonNaming {
  def apply(property: String): String = property.capitalize

  override val toString = &quot;PascalCase&quot;
}
</code></pre>
